 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Text: The voice of the drums\nIn Grandfather Ndi's village, music is everywhere. The talking drum sends messages from hill to hill: a birth, a death, a meeting at the palace. The balafon, made of wooden keys over calabashes, plays at weddings. Women sing work songs as they hoe the fields, and the rhythm makes the work lighter. At funerals, mournful songs comfort the family. “Our music is our history book,” Grandfather says. “The songs tell where we came from and teach the young how to behave.” Today, young artists mix the old rhythms — bikutsi, makossa, njang, bend skin — with guitars and computers, and Cameroonian music is heard all over the world.\n\nSome Cameroonian music styles: makossa (Douala), bikutsi (Centre, Beti), bend skin (West), njang (North West), assiko (Bassa), mangambeu (West).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Why does Grandfather say “Our music is our history book”?",
  "steps": [
   "The text says the songs tell where the people came from.",
   "They also teach the young how to behave, like a book."
  ],
  "answer": "Because the songs tell the people's history and teach the young.",
  "checks": []
 },
